#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176024 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175879 :question/id #uuid "40a301b7-9eaa-59e1-bcb0-4a35fecbff69" :question/math-academy-id "e-9551" :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/1e/1e78bf74df5607a1d80b3bd51b3222505e3bb201817448a7a5ebe5b0a73cffa9.svg)\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle y^2+\\sin x, \\: 3xy-\\cos y  \\right\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (y^2+\\sin x) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + (3xy-\\cos y) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= y^2+\\sin x$ and $Q(x,y)= 3xy-\\cos y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dx+(xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0\\leq r \\leq 2,\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi  \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $r^2\\sin\\theta.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "695a4336-df48-56c9-8472-c2f51a496819" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9211e1af-2c66-5f2d-9047-11aa8e63b870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r - r^{3}\\sin 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174125} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d2e78d-20ab-5f92-81f4-4061dc11ec75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{\\cos θ} - \\sin^{2} θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174126} {:answer/id #uuid "029cc37d-1300-55e1-a1eb-c0396c774c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r - r^{2}\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174127} {:answer/id #uuid "1f508d93-fe49-5796-a8ee-e2f2e93f9e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r + r^{2}\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174128} {:answer/id #uuid "63a45ad0-78f6-598a-9b00-e8310310981b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "re^{\\cos θ} - r\\sin^{2} θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174129}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9608a74f-b086-5f31-b192-ae95683f2fa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174124}] :question/id #uuid "e77d34a7-9f25-5206-895c-5f4fa0d1f689" :question/math-academy-id "q-125716" :question/problem "Given that\n$∮_(C)F \\cdot dr = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}AAAAAAAAdrdθ$,\nwhere $F(x, y) = ⟨xy^{2}, x + e^{\\cos y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95640d76-c966-5192-a081-34cc37eff21f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416089} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfeea07-4dc9-5945-8f32-4ba04d4c39db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416093} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0c1485-b37d-59e9-8042-95dc6071d26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416094} {:answer/id #uuid "a7529453-a85e-5454-aaea-6763faa38e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416096} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f2f158-1952-54d5-b338-cc98999f91b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95640d76-c966-5192-a081-34cc37eff21f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416089} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8487d6d-ade8-5e6b-aa72-6dcb7d480679" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416108}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bdd86cce-9e5b-5a18-9af7-54a7f6efd03e" :question/math-academy-id "q-41469" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/45/459a51f3c408a9c647b3be6b978267a6ce724e747118fbe897275c370d2bb6d6.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-2y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-2y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}r-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e93c4c-784f-5084-ab8a-83b41a9c3b12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259887} {:answer/id #uuid "1cbd94e5-4741-54cd-91f5-397f8394cacd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260121} {:answer/id #uuid "26253928-a96d-5ca2-ada8-659d943f2a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260209} {:answer/id #uuid "bc9de10a-a9df-5704-b2e5-316ac970136e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261539} {:answer/id #uuid "d06bbafa-a9ef-5c6e-ae57-d690593cedb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc9de10a-a9df-5704-b2e5-316ac970136e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261539} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b35c4ee-3674-511e-b02e-606a2c68404b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262230}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d73aed01-512e-5f02-83f5-67f605d164e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126526" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(x+1)\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/e6/e69bc348e36b05955d1eaa9eda4516afe137056ead176a46a4fc11bb887ed166.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=xy$ and $Q(x,y)=x+1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(x+1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262628 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b383de-3cfc-58c9-9df3-f58fbe8d7752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259884} {:answer/id #uuid "27f0dae3-025f-5ca9-b20c-451a7f1885e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260225} {:answer/id #uuid "5a872dfd-1e43-501e-90a1-8c43483bc0df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260649} {:answer/id #uuid "7cdd8636-0e7b-56ac-90ac-1af7ffaabbd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260958} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e7086f-0275-5f12-b2e7-d454e0bef91d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a872dfd-1e43-501e-90a1-8c43483bc0df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260649} :answer-field/id #uuid "d584d815-5b73-56a5-8b00-752a8a746831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262493}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ee4972b-d705-5cec-8dc7-f572de324a82" :question/math-academy-id "q-126542" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}y\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/5f/5f7d53fb7692663cca39f718491b4dccb6244031e2b7c75f766b7a8340696d64.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2x-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-2x-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}-2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e498e93-5b24-5605-b258-5e7001367f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260845} {:answer/id #uuid "7d508f6f-4b62-5ad5-b049-acd045b555fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260965} {:answer/id #uuid "c62c0d43-3607-57f6-bbfe-2ed08e93ae7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261621} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bf830d-1446-55f2-9cff-18bc217fc8e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261631} {:answer/id #uuid "e2636c40-db77-5979-a0a0-94fab8a3c54c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c62c0d43-3607-57f6-bbfe-2ed08e93ae7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{r}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261621} :answer-field/id #uuid "59816d41-8481-5201-be15-dbee5b961603" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262277}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db4bf9cd-fbd1-5e73-9e78-d4f96b20828d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126567" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3})\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3e/3e378b8f7731dbdbc44c232e9b8703067f39a5b3f836934afcbacb57d47972b2.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={y}^{3}+{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{3}+{e}^{x})\\,dx+(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(-3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-3\\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(-3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{5}-3{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405036 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b21e75e6-c690-5426-9022-4e65c4cd597f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405038} {:answer/id #uuid "858df234-13ff-5820-8cad-fd7b874bd17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405039} {:answer/id #uuid "132a6cae-f594-52cb-8c0a-5014dac8b218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405040} {:answer/id #uuid "289d6efd-e337-519a-9f4f-3e9ef2eb9804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405041} {:answer/id #uuid "5e407d05-c2a1-5b46-ab86-013ef5134295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e407d05-c2a1-5b46-ab86-013ef5134295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405042} :answer-field/id #uuid "18f53d24-ffcb-51fb-8ef8-920f533f7ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405037}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9b65d2f-1d10-5cc6-91ba-5ed12d5b8509" :question/math-academy-id "q-126539" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green's theorem and changing to polar coordinates $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with the integrand simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "Green's theorem states that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c2/c2718471780b5ea4f4b0298624d14a22efb317ef4e8302b9269eeeec9449ca0d.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4xy-2xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2\\cdot r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "017f8f24-8529-55cc-a1a8-41dc999d12f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405045} {:answer/id #uuid "71c0e048-b5e5-51cc-8ee4-8a54d22a60bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405046} {:answer/id #uuid "53d65de8-c1f0-596d-875a-4aa5f454bb9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405047} {:answer/id #uuid "0592c862-0b50-54dc-acf8-8691c79146ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405048} {:answer/id #uuid "bea2f9bc-178b-5408-a4cc-86ade476f6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bea2f9bc-178b-5408-a4cc-86ade476f6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405049} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca86920-5215-52e0-b0cb-aabc47b3bb45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405044}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88dc642a-08f7-5754-86d1-6d7942af6e8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126564" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5},\\,x+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green's theorem and changing to polar coordinates $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, with the integrand simplified pointwise before integration?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5}$ and $Q(x,y)=x+2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5})\\,dx+(x+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}r-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ff21cb-9e33-53f3-b627-a88a79716c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416073} {:answer/id #uuid "3abf3164-1e19-59b8-a518-c866ee98eb86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416079} {:answer/id #uuid "7406e360-d054-59fe-8f8f-ff8531ac1adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416086} {:answer/id #uuid "989f1950-08af-5b66-a148-ca15e1457577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416091} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b8980a-616f-5aa2-877a-5c0ee10376d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7406e360-d054-59fe-8f8f-ff8531ac1adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416086} :answer-field/id #uuid "634f078a-6dcb-548c-8f80-dad54be93eec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416105}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d616e0c6-f6b2-5a83-83cf-32e007120166" :question/math-academy-id "q-126569" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨y(x-1),\\,x(y-1)⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/b9/b95320928a5e7482f9ba2b8bb1d1499950710545b4c7273b7f70dfe5ab8c84b8.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=y(x-1)$ and $Q(x,y)=x(y-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y(x-1)\\,dx+x(y-1)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((y-1)-(x-1))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Line Integral Along a Circular Path as a Double Integral Using Green's Theorem"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176047 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175693 :question/id #uuid "70569afd-42c4-5e5f-8397-4fbd873eb8e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-11002" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\cos x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The range of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\n- The function has minimum values at $x =n\\pi.$\n\n- The function intersects the $x$-axis at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} +\\pi n.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos{x}.$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The minimum value is $-1,$ and the maximum value is $1.$ Therefore, the range is $[-1,1].$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has a minimum at $x=\\pi$ and the period is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, the minimum values occur at $\\pi+2n\\pi.$\n\n- Statement III is true. These are the zeros of the cosine curve. The cosine curve has zeros at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f349e4de-9f9e-5e92-9b81-db52ad4ba2fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174829} {:answer/id #uuid "0a68b6d8-5320-59e1-af7b-97987406a8dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174830} {:answer/id #uuid "0a8dae36-2ef6-5605-a493-657582045b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174831} {:answer/id #uuid "2d98a1ad-215c-5402-be54-ee893673f7e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174832} {:answer/id #uuid "539d3879-ca1d-5553-a2b6-dec799b9e7c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174833}] :answer-field/id #uuid "27db85be-39d8-55f8-966f-ff3e095cc9ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174828}] :question/id #uuid "3291f5a6-141c-577f-9e01-4f06e16f89ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-27015" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\cos x$? Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n1. The function has maximum values at $x = \\frac{π}{2} + nπ$.\n2. The function is zero at $x = \\frac{3π}{2}$.\n3. The range of the function is $[- π, π]$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd92769-ca0a-56e4-a143-8bf29d335b41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174835} {:answer/id #uuid "18288890-1a37-5a5f-8cf7-6d8a409b142c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174836} {:answer/id #uuid "28226114-2e37-5a12-9208-11e6e4343147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174837} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1e17e2-a2fe-5af9-94a2-2e198a99d6ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174838} {:answer/id #uuid "77fefe57-810f-5db3-8e76-06d7c2de4ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174839}] :answer-field/id #uuid "09c7beaa-f9fa-5209-96bf-7daa682b65ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174834}] :question/id #uuid "4431905d-f46d-548a-987b-b6164fd2cc5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-27021" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\cos x$? Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n1. The zeros of the function are $x = \\frac{π}{2} + nπ$.\n2. The function has maximum values at $x = \\frac{3π}{2} + 2nπ$.\n3. The range of $y = \\cos x$ is $[- 1, 0]$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbd74cf7-7832-