ulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "232fc51c-a784-5a0a-ab11-96190800b2c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-103307" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=\\frac{1}{3},$ for some value of $x,$ find the value of $12tan⁡(x+5\\pi )-6.$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for any integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡x=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡x=tan⁡(x+5\\cdot \\pi )=tan⁡(x+5\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $tan⁡x=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get that $tan⁡(x+5\\pi )=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n12tan⁡(x+5\\pi )-6=12(\\frac{1}{3})-6=4-6=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a2efa17-ff90-5110-b52d-af24637f4842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235979} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8cca99-6d65-568c-a0f8-a0fd7fe46ea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236118} {:answer/id #uuid "707b921b-1acb-53f3-8f3a-b5470f3888af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236530} {:answer/id #uuid "a845e9d4-c867-568d-aa25-63440fc514dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236870} {:answer/id #uuid "aa563b5d-37dd-55e3-947b-a55588cc5905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f8cca99-6d65-568c-a0f8-a0fd7fe46ea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236118} :answer-field/id #uuid "14995e9f-f94a-5811-a957-cbe2b6653873" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237464}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89698ed1-75aa-53ec-b284-4e5a8a12ecbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-103312" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=16,$ for some value of $x,$ find the value of $\\frac{3}{4}tan⁡(x-9\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for any integer $n,$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡x=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-9$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡x=tan⁡(x+(-9)\\cdot \\pi )=tan⁡(x-9\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $tan⁡x=16,$ we get that $tan⁡(x-9\\pi )=16.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}tan⁡(x-9\\pi )=\\frac{3}{4}(16)=12.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ff79e6f-631d-5bfd-a1aa-fa86f14ee9be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333132} {:answer/id #uuid "18111461-7ace-5659-bab8-5208f982db48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333204} {:answer/id #uuid "4096de65-1ecc-5d30-b6da-44dcd7d64fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333598} {:answer/id #uuid "5121b2ec-f55e-536b-9787-5bf44f6de907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333730} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ea6224-cc9f-5fe3-a180-63519162e08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5121b2ec-f55e-536b-9787-5bf44f6de907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333730} :answer-field/id #uuid "39aaac3a-a375-54e9-93b9-3f8aae2090b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335350}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f32325ca-f6af-5642-b811-5b2d78cf8877" :question/math-academy-id "q-66594" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=\\sqrt{3},$ for some $x$, find the value of $tan⁡(x-5\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡x=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡x=tan⁡(x+(-5)\\cdot \\pi )=tan⁡(x-5\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $tan⁡x=\\sqrt{3},$ we get that $tan⁡(x-5\\pi )=\\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37934bed-1f9a-50c5-9a8d-e5b0b59e71a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336549} {:answer/id #uuid "4b39580e-c808-5289-b894-51d8505df304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336729} {:answer/id #uuid "5de9a8b6-2bf6-5e41-98c3-3ffaa96e81dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336881} {:answer/id #uuid "81ac5deb-9cc0-5543-8625-93f7cbf3fed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337211} {:answer/id #uuid "b2061940-30fc-5eef-a2d6-609b6aeedec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2061940-30fc-5eef-a2d6-609b6aeedec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337622} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ab98c3a-7c21-528d-8351-dfb2882c1ded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338295}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35b95266-3a2f-569f-acd3-81fb0b0052f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-70998" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=3,$ for some $x$, find the value of $tan⁡(x+5\\pi )+2.$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡x=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡x=tan⁡(x+5\\cdot \\pi )=tan⁡(x+5\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $tan⁡x=3,$ we get that $tan⁡(x+5\\pi )=3.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\ntan⁡(x+5\\pi )+2=(3)+2=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2483a9ae-953e-586e-b9ea-aa4eddbc58be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336373} {:answer/id #uuid "38fa474b-0c10-5a11-8790-80e7d2ccd89f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336567} {:answer/id #uuid "58b1ef3d-0461-54d4-bfef-0dcf4e47f62f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336843} {:answer/id #uuid "7a68b43d-b00e-5755-83a4-9a7442c7cd27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-11\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337134} {:answer/id #uuid "cb68ac70-7dcf-5366-bb7d-8472a8b471b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb68ac70-7dcf-5366-bb7d-8472a8b471b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337819} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae78200b-d5d2-5ea6-ac9b-8cafbca6c0e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338598}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "382314ca-5ab1-5780-bf33-5145bcded9fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-71002" :question/problem "If $tan⁡x=\\sqrt{2},$ for some $x$, find the value of $tan⁡(x-11\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y=tan⁡x$ is $\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡x=tan⁡(x+n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-11$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡x=tan⁡(x+(-11)\\cdot \\pi )=tan⁡(x-11\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $tan⁡x=\\sqrt{2},$ we get that $tan⁡(x-11\\pi )=\\sqrt{2}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Tangent Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176053 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175699 :question/id #uuid "12dc1c50-69bc-54d8-8d60-a7fc144c9644" :question/math-academy-id "e-11008" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the graph of $y=\\csc x?$\n\n- The function takes the value $1$ at $x = -\\dfrac {\\pi} 2 + 2 \\pi n,$ where $n$ is an integer\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty,+\\infty)$\n\n- The function has zeroes at $x = 0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2 \\pi,\\dots$" :question/worked-solution "Let's have a look at the graph of $y=\\csc x.$\n\n![](images/a9/a92d46319d381f0a8cab7823021b3b7e9b5c030ad964586b8e72129a2cd5abe7.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The cosecant takes the value $1$ at $x = \\dfrac {\\pi} 2 + 2 \\pi n.$ Therefore, statement I is false.\n\n- The range of $y = \\csc x$ is $(-\\infty,-1] \\cup [1, \\infty).$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- The cosecant graph does not intersect the $x$-axis. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4d9a5b4-143b-5f70-96af-c54653decc87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175061} {:answer/id #uuid "bc66d19e-5e0e-5472-94a8-e7027abf7aa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175062} {:answer/id #uuid "d19b760d-4b8d-5e73-9344-5302538b3f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175063} {:answer/id #uuid "d982c693-68ac-55c9-a06f-bf9fdb410dce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175064} {:answer/id #uuid "0d82be89-281b-59c1-8ba2-fc6631fb56f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175065}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ea36c4-9c29-57d9-8e19-0b623c6933dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175060}] :question/id #uuid "5961bc49-07f6-5191-b949-28358553d498" :question/math-academy-id "q-42912" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\csc x$? Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\n1. The point $(π,-1)$ is not on the graph.\n2. The function has a vertical asymptotes at $x = nπ$.\n3. The value $y = 2$ is in the range of the function."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c602a5b-2bb3-53e8-8c57-c44d7943c842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175427} {:answer/id #uuid "94423566-84d6-59e2-b948-35feec5648f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175428} {:answer/id #uuid "33042c98-c1ca-58b5-9e13-9c601202f8ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175429} {:answer/id #uuid "356c01c6-290b-5a31-b436-001d19f505a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175430} {:answer/id #uuid "5f945eef-c736-5ead-9303-434fe3de6aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175431}] :answer-field/id #uuid "44f9590b-10c1-5111-9072-2294da633713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175426}] :question/id #uuid "82d5c89d-6391-5689-957e-6be71174a415" :question/math-academy-id "q-65310" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\csc x$? Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\n1. The domain of the function is all real numbers except $x = nπ$.\n2. The function has a zero at $x = \\frac{π}{2}$.\n3. The function takes the value $-1$ at $x =-\\frac{π}{2} + 2nπ$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d36593-75d4-519d-80e3-889e4ae17035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235830} {:answer/id #uuid "769024b7-4204-5e89-bc5a-680d454811d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236560} {:answer/id #uuid "b71e7e30-fa35-5b8a-b30b-27e146881fcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236979} {:answer/id #uuid "f18fe270-14b9-57b0-badb-0ac47a2b4d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237339} {:answer/id #uuid "f47bb03f-7102-529c-83bb-ebc037b3713d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f18fe270-14b9-57b0-badb-0ac47a2b4d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237339} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f8736d1-5cde-5191-9d87-b4dcebf146f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237608}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55643e28-3c44-53e1-a6da-d559974acbda" :question/math-academy-id "q-103811" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$\n\n- The domain of the function is all positive real numbers.\n- $x=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote of the function.\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-4\\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+6\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The function's domain is all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi $, such as $0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,….$ Hence, the statement I is false.\n\n- The lines $x=n\\pi $ for any integer $n,$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. Hence, statement II is false.\n\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi .$ So, $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-4\\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+6\\pi ).$ Hence, statement III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238056 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161c32fd-bb0c-5c3a-9a70-2e38ff080ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235952} {:answer/id #uuid "1c9c0b4a-86ce-59cd-bea8-fa20dd7847c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236003} {:answer/id #uuid "786030d4-25c9-5b36-b415-912b55a11f1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236572} {:answer/id #uuid "aeae4205-e6c3-5d0f-9138-98e42125bc1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236929} {:answer/id #uuid "b43c6310-9354-5206-886d-6239c7a82269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c9c0b4a-86ce-59cd-bea8-fa20dd7847c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236003} :answer-field/id #uuid "cace984a-b193-5b87-8192-dfb23054b530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237703}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "048ceaff-1e4b-5807-8d50-fab96c1502f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-103814" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$ Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\n- $y=1$ is the minimum value of the function. $$\n- $x=9\\pi $ is a vertical asymptote of the function.\n- The function takes the value $1$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/06/066fe6ce8a65927436d64753ec4e61bfea257405e4b2f81554ac8341572bcf15.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )$, therefore the function doesn't have a minimum value. Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The lines $x=n\\pi $ for any integer $n$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. Hence statement II is true.\n\n- The cosecant takes the value $1$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .$ Hence statement III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2948f95d-327e-5b2a-ae44-d052e460ef7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333370} {:answer/id #uuid "4f47b504-2866-5cc0-a5de-126c425a1f79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333715} {:answer/id #uuid "546b3906-9278-5c88-9389-7d8f880155e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333760} {:answer/id #uuid "c041a1ee-404c-5c71-88e2-cb1195daeaea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334655} {:answer/id #uuid "f1045889-a661-5c2b-b9ef-ba20d8522b18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c041a1ee-404c-5c71-88e2-cb1195daeaea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334655} :answer-field/id #uuid "8aa416a5-c692-5dfb-b0e9-591568885d69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335505}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7573ddef-f834-5ca7-911d-588bad3a2d0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-65307" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$\n\nI. The domain of the function is all real numbers.\n\nII. $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a vertical asymptote of the function.\n\nIII. The graph of the function doesn't intersect the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The domain of the function is all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi $, such as $0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,….$ Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The lines $x=n\\pi ,$ for any integer $n,$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. Hence, statement II is false.\n\n- The graph of the function doesn't intersect the $y$-axis, as $x=0$ is not in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$ Hence, statement III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1abfdb94-0b90-5fec-9454-e8ca2b729611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333236} {:answer/id #uuid "1f44d8a0-aa03-55c7-b38e-42f488e8b7a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333270} {:answer/id #uuid "4600ff20-ec76-571f-b416-17d4739b6ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333638} {:answer/id #uuid "50e29691-496c-5d3e-bddc-fccb10414b1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333727} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd338d2-6208-532a-8aee-5976f2d53a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1abfdb94-0b90-5fec-9454-e8ca2b729611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333236} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8851140-7924-5505-a58d-6ccd9f73c016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335608}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69d68c82-75d2-569e-bc5a-a35be8a71a62" :question/math-academy-id "q-65308" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$\n\n- The range of the function is all real numbers.\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\pi ).$\n- $x=5\\pi $ is in the domain of the function." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).$ Hence, statement I is false.\n\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi ,$ so $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2n\\pi )$ for any integer $n.$ Substituing $n=1,$ we get $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\pi ).$ Hence, Statement II is true.\n\n- The function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is defined for all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi ,$ so $5\\pi $ is not in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$ Hence, statement III is false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb60cb3-357c-5e48-b8d1-0086b493c7eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399400} {:answer/id #uuid "19ccc92a-6243-5f86-923a-b5c24bd00fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399401} {:answer/id #uuid "ce053c47-98cc-5de1-940a-50290e3cb268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399402} {:answer/id #uuid "db658c44-2180-5d2a-b3df-00809159cb77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399403} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8ab29b-c5b0-5fbd-9665-137b0c595045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb60cb3-357c-5e48-b8d1-0086b493c7eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399400} :answer-field/id #uuid "df865d93-f1c0-5ed8-a22e-b99b64edd838" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399399}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4383ecaa-1c57-5705-8853-cba2341fc9fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-103812" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x?$\n\nI. The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1].$\nII. $y=-1$ is the maximum value of the function.\nIII. $x=15\\pi $ is in the domain." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b80c29b8f9f4d33aae94fc287d95b97cc5865b36ecc98f4f41e625dfc842e1.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- The range of the function is $(-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ),$ therefore the function doesn't have a maximum value. Hence, statements I and II are both false.\n\n- The function's domain is all real numbers except the integer multiples of $\\pi $, such as $0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,….$ Hence, statement III is false."}] :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176054 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175700 :question/id #uuid "a1713375-241f-5a53-a109-3c3d0f917411" :question/math-academy-id "e-11009" :question/problem "If $\\csc x = 8,$ then what is $\\csc(x-8\\pi)?$" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\csc x$ is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc{x} = \\csc{(x+2n\\pi)} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-4$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot (-4)\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-8\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\csc{x} = 8,$ then $\\csc{(x-8\\pi)} = 8.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37434c3e-2269-5b08-94c5-7516df7879b2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a68b899f-c449-5971-9d3e-73a538c1eadf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175457} {:answer/id #uuid "b340d194-ca79-5c00-828c-ee58e2737378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175458} {:answer/id #uuid "dd4e14ce-5c16-57f1-805f-dd739f311334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(1 + π)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175459} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf5c125-4788-5377-889c-2a88a377d169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 + 2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175460} {:answer/id #uuid "6429dc36-e0a3-578d-be23-66e10cceaad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175461}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c13c4676-eb3b-5a2d-be59-3bfd1edf0361" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175456}] :question/id #uuid "c6e6a2a5-fb7f-5830-ba84-c06c6a0dfa7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-66472" :question/problem "If $\\csc x = 4$, then what is $\\csc (x + 4π)$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176554 :question/answer-fields [{:an